  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 122 AM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nMoving into the weekend, the speed of a propagating frontal boundary \noff of the trough that is swinging across the central and eastern US \nwill be a big player in the amount of rainfall that we may see over \nthe weekend. Ensembles plus much of the deterministic guidance are \ngenerally coming together at this point with the front pushing into \ncentral and perhaps the northernmost portions of the South Florida \nforecast area Saturday evening and overnight into Sunday. The \nsteadiest rain would be expected directly ahead of the front, \nleading to the most widespread rainfall likely to occur on Saturday \nbefore the front pushes out the deeper moisture from portions of the \nregion. Potential rainfall amounts are still heavily uncertain, but \nthe latest 90th percentile 24-hour QPF is between 2-4\" for Saturday \nand lower for Sunday, suggesting that even in a worst case scenario \nwe may only need to be concerned for localized flooding. \nNevertheless, we still need another day or two to get a more \naccurate perception of how much rain is possible in different time \nspans this weekend.\n\nFollowing the front stalling out late this weekend and into early \nnext week, there is better agreement across the ensemble guidance \nthat a steep pressure gradient will set up over the entire state of \nFlorida, bringing with it a jump in easterly winds. The exact timing \nof the onset of winds here will be tied to the speed and direction \nof the high pressure system building behind the front which is not \ncompletely resolved yet, but less widespread rain is expected as we \nmove into next week and extending into the middle of the week.",
